![high](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/high-priority.svg)

The `load_u32x4` helper performs a 128-bit vectorized load by JIT-compiling a cast of `base + offset` to `const uint4*`. In CUDA, dereferencing a `uint4*` requires the memory address to be 16-byte aligned. Since `base` is a `const unsigned int*` (which is 4-byte aligned), `offset` must be a multiple of 4 to guarantee 16-byte alignment. If `offset` is not a multiple of 4, this JIT-compiled kernel will trigger an illegal memory access (misaligned address) and crash the GPU. Please document this alignment requirement clearly or add a safe fallback.

```
__device__ __forceinline__ void load_u32x4(const unsigned int* base, unsigned int offset,
                                            unsigned int& a, unsigned int& b,
                                            unsigned int& c, unsigned int& d) {
    // Note: base + offset must be 16-byte aligned (offset must be a multiple of 4)
    const uint4 v = *reinterpret_cast<const uint4*>(base + offset);
    a = v.x; b = v.y; c = v.z; d = v.w;
}
```

![medium](https://www.gstatic.com/codereviewagent/medium-priority.svg)

If `count` is provided and is larger than `packed.len() * 32`, the loop will attempt to access `packed[word]` where `word >= packed.len()`, causing an out-of-bounds panic. Clamping `total_bits` to `packed.len() * BINARY_VALUES_PER_WORD` prevents any potential out-of-bounds panics.

```suggestion
pub fn unpack_binary(packed: &[u32], count: Option<usize>) -> Vec<bool> {
    let total_bits = count
        .unwrap_or(packed.len() * BINARY_VALUES_PER_WORD)
        .min(packed.len() * BINARY_VALUES_PER_WORD);
    let mut out = Vec::with_capacity(total_bits);
    for i in 0..total_bits {
        let word = i / BINARY_VALUES_PER_WORD;
        let bit = i % BINARY_VALUES_PER_WORD;
        out.push((packed[word] >> bit) & 1 != 0);
    }
    out
}
```

